To

The Admissions Committee,

I am pleased to recommend Anirudh Bocha for admission to your graduate program. For the past two years, I have served as his Honours advisor at the Speech Processing Lab in IIIT Hyderabad, supervising his research across four semesters. Over this period, I have watched him mature from an undergraduate building his foundation in deep learning into an independent researcher. He is technically strong, intellectually curious, and well prepared to engage with open-ended research problems.

Anirudh’s early work in my laboratory examined acoustic representations for speech tasks, where he evaluated spectral features and speech encoder architectures by comparing their trade-offs between accuracy and computational efficiency. Rather than treating experiments as routine trials, he approached them systematically probing why different methods behaved as they did and identifying which representations held up under specific conditions. That analytical habit carried directly into his Honours research on typical and atypical speech disfluencies, where limited and variable data made subtle acoustic events difficult to capture reliably.

Rather than assuming that a speech encoder’s final layer offers the best representation for every downstream task, Anirudh studied how task-relevant information is distributed across model depth. Evaluating Wav2Vec 2.0, HuBERT, WavLM, and TERA across their transformer layers for disfluency detection, he showed that intermediate representations are far more effective at capturing fine-grained disfluencies: in particular, HuBERT’s fifth layer achieved an F1 score of 0.97, outperforming its final layer. What stood out to me was how he used these findings to challenge the conventional assumption that deeper representations are always superior. His layer-wise analysis and experimental work formed a substantial contribution to our paper published at Interspeech 2025.

Alongside his research, Anirudh led a team of six undergraduate students in developing Waveform-Wizard, an open-source Python replacement for our laboratory’s MATLAB-based speech-analysis workflow. In addition to contributing directly to its core implementation, he coordinated the integration of its analysis modules into an interactive system accessible to other users. This work was presented at ICASSP 2025, demonstrating his ability to lead a broad technical effort while attending to the practical details required to make a system genuinely usable.

Beyond his published projects, Anirudh took the initiative to build reusable infrastructure for the wider laboratory, including a model-serving framework that exposed internal models through a web interface with minimal setup and a multilingual speech-recording platform with administrative quality control. Both tools extended his impact well beyond his own experiments by making our group’s day-to-day research workflow more efficient.

Across these four semesters, I have seen Anirudh grow increasingly comfortable with problems that lack an obvious solution. He examines empirical results critically, identifies where an approach falls short, and pursues principled alternatives rather than stopping as soon as an initial implementation works. Reliable, self-motivated, and committed to turning ideas into working systems, Anirudh possesses the intellect, rigor, and perseverance needed to excel in graduate school. I recommend him strongly and am confident that he will continue to develop into a capable independent researcher.

Sincerely,

Dr. Anil Kumar Vuppala
Professor
Language Technologies Research Center
International Institute of Information Technology, Hyderabad


